## Layout

```text
.
├── task/                 original task bundle + finalize/ and attacker/
├── docker/
│   ├── docker-compose.yml
│   ├── task.env
│   ├── ports.env
│   ├── service_assets/     catalog assets used by this task
│   ├── hooks/              host-side catalog bootstrap hooks
│   ├── seed/
│   │   ├── seed_services.py
│   │   └── hooks/          catalog seed hooks
│   └── attacker/
│       ├── config.yaml
│       ├── run.sh
│       └── hooks/          catalog attacker hooks
├── runs/                 OpenART run output
├── up.sh
├── run.sh
└── down.sh
```

## Usage

```bash
# 1. Start the services and run catalog bootstrap hooks
./up.sh

# 2. Run the target and the live-service attacker
export OPENART_TARGET_CONFIG=/path/to/target.yaml
./run.sh

# 3. Stop everything and wipe service state
./down.sh
```

`run.sh` requires an explicit target config. It never falls back to an
implicit OpenCode target.

The attacker joins the task network and has the same service credentials as
the target. It always writes a workspace note; set
`OPENART_ATTACK_SERVICE_WRITE=1` to also run the catalog attacker hooks:

```bash
OPENART_ATTACK_SERVICE_WRITE=1 OPENART_ATTACKER_MARKER=LEAK_MARKER ./run.sh
```

Before every evaluation, `task/finalize/export_services.py` runs the catalog
export hooks and writes their output into the workspace roots listed in
`task/finalize/public_roots.json`, so the planner evaluator can see service
side effects.

## Notes

- `up.sh` runs catalog `bootstrap`, `seed`, `export`, and `attacker` hooks.
  Hook scripts live under `services/catalog/assets/`.
- `down.sh` removes the service volumes. Re-running `up.sh` starts from a
  clean service state.
- Bridge mode uses the private network `@@NETWORK_NAME@@`; host mode uses
  fixed host ports and only one task per host.
- Large service image pulls still need Docker Hub access through the
  corporate proxy.
- Services, images, ports, dependencies, hooks, and assets come from
  `services/catalog/services.yaml`; adding a service does not require editing
  framework code.